<h1>Home Inspection Checklist for Military Buyers</h1>

<p>The home inspection is your opportunity to identify problems before purchasing, potentially saving tens of thousands of dollars in unexpected repairs. Military families often skip professional inspections to save money, but this false economy frequently costs far more later. A thorough home inspection costing $400-$600 can identify $5,000-$20,000 in needed repairs, allowing you to renegotiate or walk away from problematic purchases.</p>

<p>This comprehensive checklist covers what professional inspectors examine and what military buyers should understand during the inspection process. Armed with this knowledge, you'll ask informed questions and make confident decisions about your San Antonio home purchase.</p>

<h2>Before the Inspection: Preparation</h2>

<p>Select a professional home inspector certified through the Texas Real Estate Commission (TREC). Ask for references from previous military clients and verify they have experience with homes in your target area. Different San Antonio neighborhoods have different common issues—an inspector familiar with Spring Branch homes knows what to look for.</p>

<p>Plan to attend your inspection personally if possible. Inspectors appreciate engaged buyers who ask questions and learn about their potential home. If you're relocating from overseas and can't attend in person, request video calls during the inspection or detailed written reports with photos.</p>

<p>Inform your inspector if you have specific concerns about the property. If the home is older, request focus on aging systems. If you're concerned about foundation issues (common in some San Antonio areas), ask the inspector to pay particular attention to cracks and settling.</p>

<h2>Structural Systems Inspection</h2>

<h3>Foundation and Structure</h3>

<p>The foundation is the most critical component. Look for:</p>

<ul>
<li>Vertical cracks in foundation walls (hairline is normal; large cracks indicate problems)</li>
<li>Stair-step cracks in brick, indicating foundation settlement</li>
<li>Water stains on foundation (indicating past or present water problems)</li>
<li>Gaps between walls and foundation</li>
<li>Bowing or leaning exterior walls</li>
<li>Uneven floors or doors that don't close properly (possible foundation issues)</li>
</ul>

<p>San Antonio's expansive clay soil can cause foundation movement, particularly during droughts. Many San Antonio homes have had foundation repairs. If evidence of repair exists, ask for documentation of work performed and warranties provided.</p>

<h3>Roof Condition</h3>

<p>The roof protects everything beneath it. Inspectors examine:</p>

<ul>
<li>Shingle condition—curling, cracking, or missing shingles</li>
<li>Shingle age—most composition shingles last 15-20 years</li>
<li>Flashing condition around penetrations (chimneys, vents, pipes)</li>
<li>Gutter and downspout condition</li>
<li>Signs of leaking in attic (water stains, mold)</li>
<li>Roof slope and proper drainage</li>
<li>Visible moss or algae growth (possible moisture issues)</li>
</ul>

<p>Roof replacement costs $8,000-$15,000 depending on size and materials. If the roof is near the end of its life, factor replacement costs into your negotiation.</p>

<h2>Water and Moisture Systems</h2>

<h3>Plumbing and Water Supply</h3>

<p>Inspectors check:</p>

<ul>
<li>Water pressure at all fixtures</li>
<li>Hot water availability and heating capability</li>
<li>Evidence of leaks under sinks and around toilets</li>
<li>Toilet functionality and water quality</li>
<li>Sump pump presence and functionality (if basement exists)</li>
<li>Water heater age and condition (typical life span 10-15 years)</li>
<li>Signs of hard water buildup or sediment</li>
</ul>

<p>San Antonio's water is relatively hard, and some homes have water softener systems that may need maintenance. Ask about any water quality issues in the neighborhood.</p>

<h3>Drainage and Foundation Protection</h3>

<p>Proper drainage protects foundations from water damage:</p>

<ul>
<li>Gutters and downspouts directing water away from foundation</li>
<li>Grading around home sloping away from foundation</li>
<li>Evidence of water intrusion in basement or crawl space</li>
<li>Signs of standing water or dampness</li>
<li>Proper drainage system functionality if installed</li>
</ul>

<h2>HVAC Systems</h2>

<h3>Air Conditioning and Heating</h3>

<p>San Antonio's extreme heat makes AC functionality critical. Inspectors verify:</p>

<ul>
<li>AC system age and condition (typical life span 15-20 years)</li>
<li>Proper cooling and temperature control</li>
<li>Heating system functionality (though less critical in San Antonio)</li>
<li>Thermostat type and functionality</li>
<li>Ductwork condition and evidence of leaking</li>
<li>Filter accessibility and replacement records</li>
<li>Outdoor unit condition and proper clearance</li>
<li>Refrigerant levels and system efficiency</li>
</ul>

<p>AC replacement costs $5,000-$10,000, making system age a significant inspection factor. If the system is 15+ years old, expect replacement within the next 5 years.</p>

<h3>Ventilation</h3>

<p>Proper ventilation affects air quality and system efficiency:</p>

<ul>
<li>Range hood ventilation to exterior</li>
<li>Bathroom exhaust fans properly vented</li>
<li>Attic ventilation adequate for heat management</li>
<li>Dryer venting to exterior (not into attic)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Electrical Systems</h2>

<h3>Panel and Service</h3>

<p>The electrical panel distributes power throughout the home:</p>

<ul>
<li>Panel amperage adequate for home size (typically 100-200 amps)</li>
<li>Breaker condition and labeling</li>
<li>Proper grounding and bonding</li>
<li>Evidence of amateur or improper electrical work</li>
<li>Outdated or recalled panel types (FPE, Zinsco panels have safety concerns)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Outlets and Fixtures</h3>

<p>Inspectors test electrical functionality:</p>

<ul>
<li>GFCI outlets in kitchens, bathrooms, and wet areas</li>
<li>Proper outlet grounding (three-prong functionality)</li>
<li>Adequate outlet quantity in rooms</li>
<li>Smoke and carbon monoxide detectors present and functional</li>
<li>Light fixtures operational</li>
<li>Electrical concerns related to aluminum wiring (older homes)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Interior Systems and Components</h2>

<h3>Insulation and Ventilation</h3>

<p>Proper insulation affects energy efficiency and comfort:</p>

<ul>
<li>Attic insulation adequate (R-30 to R-49 recommended for San Antonio)</li>
<li>Wall insulation present and adequate</li>
<li>Basement or crawl space insulation</li>
<li>Evidence of thermal imaging or energy loss</li>
</ul>

<h3>Windows and Doors</h3>

<p>Windows and doors affect energy efficiency and security:</p>

<ul>
<li>Window operation and sealing</li>
<li>Evidence of broken seals or condensation between panes</li>
<li>Caulking and weatherstripping condition</li>
<li>Door hardware and lock functionality</li>
<li>Proper weather sealing around entries</li>
</ul>

<h3>Interior Walls and Ceilings</h3>

<p>Interior surfaces reveal moisture and structural issues:</p>

<ul>
<li>Evidence of water stains on ceilings or walls</li>
<li>Mold or mildew growth (indicates moisture problems)</li>
<li>Cracks in drywall or plaster</li>
<li>Evidence of pest damage or infestation</li>
<li>Paint condition and coverage of problem areas</li>
</ul>

<h3>Flooring</h3>

<p>Flooring condition affects both appearance and home function:</p>

<ul>
<li>Hardwood floor condition and finish</li>
<li>Carpet cleanliness and condition</li>
<li>Tile condition and grout integrity</li>
<li>Evidence of water damage or moisture beneath flooring</li>
<li>Subfloor condition if visible</li>
</ul>

<h2>Kitchen and Bathrooms</h2>

<h3>Kitchen Systems</h3>

<p>Kitchens receive heavy use and show wear:</p>

<ul>
<li>Appliance condition and functionality (age of major appliances)</li>
<li>Cabinet condition and hardware</li>
<li>Countertop condition and damage</li>
<li>Sink functionality and drainage</li>
<li>Proper ventilation for cooking</li>
<li>Flooring condition and water damage risk</li>
</ul>

<h3>Bathroom Systems</h3>

<p>Bathrooms are moisture-prone areas requiring careful inspection:</p>

<ul>
<li>Toilet functionality and condition</li>
<li>Sink and faucet functionality</li>
<li>Shower and tub condition, caulking, and functionality</li>
<li>Tile or surround condition and water damage</li>
<li>Ventilation adequacy</li>
<li>Evidence of mold or mildew (water intrusion indicator)</li>
<li>Flooring condition and water damage</li>
</ul>

<h2>Exterior and Site Conditions</h2>

<h3>Exterior Walls and Siding</h3>

<p>The exterior protects against weather:</p>

<ul>
<li>Siding material condition (brick, wood, vinyl, etc.)</li>
<li>Evidence of damage, gaps, or deterioration</li>
<li>Proper caulking and sealant condition</li>
<li>Evidence of water damage or staining</li>
<li>Paint condition and peeling</li>
<li>Brick mortar condition (critical in San Antonio heat cycles)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Lot and Landscaping</h3>

<p>Site conditions affect foundation protection and aesthetic appeal:</p>

<ul>
<li>Proper lot drainage away from home</li>
<li>Tree condition and potential hazards</li>
<li>Landscaping encroaching on foundation or utilities</li>
<li>Fence condition and property line clarity</li>
<li>Driveway and walkway condition</li>
<li>Evidence of erosion or improper grading</li>
</ul>

<h2>Red Flags and Serious Concerns</h2>

<p>Some inspection findings are deal-breakers or require serious negotiation:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Foundation damage</strong>—Large cracks, bowing walls, or severe settlement issues</li>
<li><strong>Roof failure</strong>—Extensive damage or near-end-of-life condition requiring imminent replacement</li>
<li><strong>Active water intrusion</strong>—Wet walls, mold growth, or standing water</li>
<li><strong>HVAC failure</strong>—Non-functional AC in San Antonio heat</li>
<li><strong>Outdated electrical</strong>—Aluminum wiring, recalled panels, or inadequate service</li>
<li><strong>Structural issues</strong>—Evidence of settling, deterioration, or improper construction</li>
<li><strong>Pest infestation</strong>—Active evidence of termites, rodents, or other pests</li>
<li><strong>Mold growth</strong>—Evidence of extensive mold indicating systemic moisture problems</li>
</ul>

<p>These issues don't automatically mean walking away, but they require professional repair estimates and serious negotiation with sellers.</p>

<h2>After the Inspection: Next Steps</h2>

<p>Request detailed written reports with photos and descriptions of all findings. Categorize repairs as critical (safety or structural), necessary (system failure soon), or cosmetic (appearance only). Request repair estimates for critical items.</p>

<p>Armed with inspection findings, you can negotiate with the seller for repairs, credits at closing, or price reduction. Many sellers prefer credits at closing, allowing you to choose contractors and manage repairs on your timeline.</p>

<p>If inspection reveals significant problems, you can invoke your inspection contingency to renegotiate or withdraw from the purchase. This contingency protects you from acquiring properties with hidden problems.</p>

<h2>Common San Antonio Home Issues</h2>

<p>Military families should be particularly aware of issues common in San Antonio:</p>

<ul>
<li>Foundation movement from expansive clay and drought cycles</li>
<li>Aging AC systems struggling with extreme heat</li>
<li>Hard water and mineral buildup requiring softeners</li>
<li>Moisture intrusion from heavy summer rains</li>
<li>Brick mortar deterioration from freeze-thaw cycles</li>
<li>Improper grading causing foundation drainage issues</li>
</ul>

<h2>Inspector Questions to Ask</h2>

<p>During your inspection, ask:</p>

<ul>
<li>"What's the remaining life expectancy of major systems?"</li>
<li>"What are the most significant issues in this home?"</li>
<li>"Are these common problems in homes in this area?"</li>
<li>"What repairs should occur within the next 5 years?"</li>
<li>"What costs might I anticipate for necessary repairs?"</li>
<li>"Would you buy this home? Why or why not?"</li>
</ul>

<h2>Conclusion</h2>

<p>The home inspection is your critical protection as a buyer. A thorough inspection identifies problems before you're committed to purchase, allowing informed decision-making. Military families with limited time to relocate should prioritize inspections despite scheduling pressure—knowing your home's condition prevents costly surprises and ensures you're making a sound financial decision.</p>

<p>Don't skip the inspection to save $400-$600. The potential savings far exceed the inspection cost, and the peace of mind knowing your home's condition is invaluable during military transitions.</p>

